            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous intracranial hypotension followed by dural sinus thrombosis: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>498-500</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clineuro.2010.01.010</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>SIH is an underdiagnosed condition with variable clinical and radiological presentations that leads to engorged dural sinuses and promotes stasis predisposing for dural sinus thrombosis (DST). We describe a young female patient who presented with clinical and imaging signs of SIH alone followed by a superimposed DST 10 days later. We discuss the pathophysiological mechanisms that may have led to this constellation, and review the literature to-date along with diagnostic and management implications.</AbstractText>
